.include {docs}

# go2_mapping_bringup

`go2_mapping_bringup` starts the mapping chain. It has no code: only launch files that start the
nodes, and config files that hold their settings.

**What.** A ROS 2 package with three launch files and three config files. One command starts
`go2_preprocessor`, Point-LIO and `map_builder`, gives each its settings, and connects them with
the right topic names.

**Why.** The chain has three programs from three places, and they only work together if the details
match:

- each one needs its own settings file, and a wrong node name makes ROS ignore the file without an error;
- Point-LIO reads the preprocessor's topics, and `map_builder` reads Point-LIO's topics;
- the calibration file must reach the preprocessor, or the map comes out tilted;
- the robot and the laptop must use the same DDS (the layer ROS uses to send messages), or they do not see each other.

This package fixes all of these in one place, so nobody types them by hand.

**When.** Every time you map. `tools/mapping/map.sh` calls it for you.

## Where it sits

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      map["tools/mapping/map.sh"] -->|"ros2 launch"| bring[go2_mapping_bringup]
      bring -->|starts| pre[go2_preprocessor]
      bring -->|starts| lio[Point-LIO]
      bring -->|starts| mb[map_builder]
      cal[(go2_calibration.yaml)] -->|"calibration_file"| bring
      cfg[(config folder)] --> bring

The package does not run anything itself. It tells ROS what to start. The three nodes it starts have
their own pages: [go2_preprocessor](./go2_preprocessor.qd) is done, `map_builder` comes next.

## What is inside

| File | Does |
|---|---|
| `launch/mapping.launch.py` | The main one. Starts the preprocessor and Point-LIO, and `map_builder` when it runs on the robot |
| `launch/map_builder.launch.py` | Starts `map_builder` alone. Included by the other two, not run by hand |
| `launch/laptop.launch.py` | The laptop side. Starts `map_builder` there when you want it there |
| `config/preprocessor.yaml` | The robot box of `go2_preprocessor` |
| `config/point_lio.yaml` | All settings of Point-LIO |
| `config/map_builder.yaml` | Voxel size and publish rates of `map_builder` |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | Installs `launch/` and `config/` so ROS can find them |
| `package.xml` | Lists the four packages it starts as run dependencies |

## Quick start

The normal way, on the robot computer. The script checks the topics, the disk and the calibration,
and then calls the launch file:

```bash
tools/mapping/map.sh
```

The launch file alone:

```bash
ros2 launch go2_mapping_bringup mapping.launch.py \
    calibration_file:=$HOME/calibration/current.yaml
```

`calibration_file` has no default. This is on purpose: without it the preprocessor would use CMU's
mount values, and the map would come out tilted. Launch stops at once if you forget it.

The same command works live and with `ros2 bag play`. All nodes use the stamps inside the messages
and not the clock, so there is no `use_sim_time` switch (Foxy's bag player has no `--clock`).

## How it works

### Who starts whom

`mapping.launch.py` is a list of actions. ROS runs them in order. The last one includes another
launch file, but only when a condition is true.

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph M["mapping.launch.py"]
        a1["1 declare calibration_file"]
        a2["2 declare map_builder_host"]
        a3["3 set RMW_IMPLEMENTATION"]
        a4["4 Node go2_preprocessor"]
        a5["5 Node point_lio"]
        a6["6 include map_builder.launch.py, if host is robot"]
      end
      a1 --> a2 --> a3 --> a4 --> a5 --> a6
      a6 -.-> B["map_builder.launch.py"]
      L["laptop.launch.py"] -.->|"if host is laptop"| B
      B --> N["Node map_builder"]

Both `mapping.launch.py` and `laptop.launch.py` include `map_builder.launch.py`. One of them has the
condition true, the other false, so `map_builder` starts exactly once. Step 3 is explained in
[Which DDS](#which-dds).

### What happens at start-up

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant U as you or map.sh
      participant L as ros2 launch
      participant P as go2_preprocessor
      participant O as point_lio
      participant B as map_builder
      U->>L: mapping.launch.py calibration_file:=...
      L->>L: set RMW_IMPLEMENTATION if the shell has none
      L->>P: start, parameters from preprocessor.yaml then the calibration file
      L->>O: start, parameters from point_lio.yaml
      L->>B: start, parameters from map_builder.yaml and output_dir
      P-->>P: log the calibration
      O-->>O: wait for init_map_size points, robot still
      B-->>B: wait for the first scan and pose

The three nodes start together, not one after the other. Nothing waits for the previous node to be
ready. This is fine because a node that has no input yet just waits.

Point-LIO needs about 3 s to start, then it takes gravity and its first map from the next seconds.
The robot must stand still for that time. `map.sh` prints KEEP THE ROBOT STILL for 10 s.

### Where the data flows

The launch files do not carry data. They only set the names so that the nodes find each other.

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      robot["Go2: /utlidar/cloud and /sportmodestate"] --> pre[go2_preprocessor]
      pre -->|"/utlidar/transformed_cloud"| lio[Point-LIO]
      pre -->|"/utlidar/transformed_imu"| lio
      lio -->|"/cloud_registered"| mb[map_builder]
      lio -->|"/aft_mapped_to_init"| mb
      mb -->|"map_builder/delta, pose"| viewer["viewer, later"]
      mb -.->|"Ctrl-C"| files[("map.pcd and trajectory.tum")]

Where the names are set:

| Connection | Set in | How |
|---|---|---|
| Go2 to preprocessor | `go2_preprocessor` code | Fixed in the code |
| Preprocessor to Point-LIO | `point_lio.yaml` | `lid_topic` and `imu_topic` |
| Point-LIO to `map_builder` | `map_builder.launch.py` | `remappings`: `cloud` becomes `/cloud_registered`, `odom` becomes `/aft_mapped_to_init` |

`map_builder` only knows the short names `cloud` and `odom`. The launch file points them at
Point-LIO. The frame of `/cloud_registered` is `camera_init`, Point-LIO's world frame.

### How the settings reach each node

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
      subgraph files["config files"]
        c1["preprocessor.yaml: key go2_preprocessor"]
        c2["calibration yaml: key go2_preprocessor"]
        c3["point_lio.yaml: key point_lio"]
        c4["map_builder.yaml: key map_builder"]
      end
      c1 --> pre[go2_preprocessor]
      c2 -->|"overrides"| pre
      c3 --> lio[point_lio]
      c4 --> mb[map_builder]
      arg["output_dir argument"] --> mb

Each file starts with a key: the **node name**. ROS gives the file to the node with that name and
ignores it for all others. If the key and the name differ, ROS says nothing and the node runs with its
defaults. This is why the launch file comments say "the node name must stay `go2_preprocessor`".

For the preprocessor two files are given. The second wins when both set a value. The robot box is in
`preprocessor.yaml` and the measured numbers are in the calibration file, so they never clash.

`output_dir` is not in `map_builder.yaml`. The launch file sets it, because `~` must mean the home
folder of the machine that runs the node, and that is only known at launch time.

### Robot or laptop

By default everything runs on the robot. You can move `map_builder` to the laptop. Point-LIO then
sends its scans over Wi-Fi.

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      s{"map_builder_host"}
      s -->|"robot, the default"| r["robot: mapping.launch.py starts all three nodes"]
      s -->|"laptop"| l1["robot: mapping.launch.py starts preprocessor and Point-LIO"]
      s -->|"laptop"| l2["laptop: laptop.launch.py starts map_builder"]

```bash
# on the robot
ros2 launch go2_mapping_bringup mapping.launch.py calibration_file:=... map_builder_host:=laptop
# on the laptop
ros2 launch go2_mapping_bringup laptop.launch.py map_builder_host:=laptop
```

The value must be **the same on both machines**. Two commands that say `robot` and `laptop` would
start two `map_builder`s (the second stops itself) or none. On the laptop with `robot`, the launch
starts nothing.

### Which DDS

DDS is the layer under ROS that sends the messages. Foxy uses Fast-RTPS by default. The robot uses
CycloneDDS. Two machines with different DDS do not talk to each other. Point-LIO also hangs on
Ctrl-C with Fast-RTPS.

Both launch files therefore set `RMW_IMPLEMENTATION` to `rmw_cyclonedds_cpp`, but only if the shell has
no value yet. A value you set yourself is kept.

### What happens on Ctrl-C

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant U as you
      participant L as ros2 launch
      participant P as go2_preprocessor
      participant O as point_lio
      participant B as map_builder
      U->>L: Ctrl-C
      L->>P: stop
      L->>O: stop
      L->>B: stop
      B->>B: save_on_exit: write map.pcd and trajectory.tum
      O-->>L: "terminate called without an active exception"

Ctrl-C reaches every node. `map_builder` writes the map into a new folder
`<output_dir>/<time of the first scan, UTC>/`. The Point-LIO message is a known flaw of its exit. It
comes after the map is saved and does not cost data.

## The settings

### Launch arguments

| Argument | Default | In | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `calibration_file` | none, required | `mapping.launch.py` | Absolute path of the `go2_calibration` output |
| `map_builder_host` | `robot` | `mapping.launch.py`, `laptop.launch.py` | Which machine runs `map_builder`. Same on both |
| `output_dir` | `~/maps` | `map_builder.launch.py` | Folder where each run gets its sub-folder |

`output_dir` is declared in `map_builder.launch.py`. Passed to `mapping.launch.py` it reaches it.

### point_lio.yaml

Taken from CMU's `utlidar.yaml`. The file has about 60 values, each with a comment. These are the ones
that differ from CMU or that you may want to change:

| Parameter | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `lid_topic`, `imu_topic` | the preprocessor's two topics | Point-LIO reads the corrected data, not the robot's |
| `extrinsic_T`, `extrinsic_R` | zero, identity | The preprocessor already turned lidar and IMU into one body frame |
| `init_map_size` | 5000 | About 0.5 s of scans. CMU's 10 builds the first map from one scan, mostly floor, and the gyro bias runs away |
| `gravity`, `gravity_align` | (0, 0, -9.81), true | The IMU is scaled to 9.81 at rest. Without gravity, Point-LIO reads it as the robot speeding up |
| `acc_norm` | 9.81 | The accelerometer unit is m/s^2 |
| `satu_acc` | 40 | The body accelerometer peaks at 29 m/s^2 at foot strikes. CMU's 30 would flag them |
| `blind` | 0.5 m | Drops lidar points closer than this. It removes the robot's own body |
| `filter_size_surf`, `filter_size_map` | 0.1 m | Voxel size of the scan and of Point-LIO's map. Smaller means finer, more memory and CPU |
| `use_imu_as_input` | false | The IMU is a measurement of the filter, which holds up in rough motion |
| `pcd_save_en` | false | Point-LIO would overwrite one file per run. `map_builder` saves each run in its own folder |

### map_builder.yaml

| Parameter | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `voxel_size` | 0.05 m | One point per voxel. On `2026-10-19_line_01`: 45,195 points at 0.05, 14,476 at 0.10 |
| `delta_rate_hz` | 2 | How often new voxels are sent |
| `pose_rate_hz` | 10 | How often the robot pose is sent |
| `full_map_rate_hz` | 0 | The whole map on a topic. 0 is off, because every message holds the whole map |
| `save_on_exit` | true | Save on Ctrl-C, so a run is not lost if nobody called the save service |

Calling the save service and then stopping gives two folders, `<time>` and `<time>_1`.

### preprocessor.yaml

Only the `robot_box`. See [go2_preprocessor](./go2_preprocessor.qd) for what the box does.

## Build and test

The package only copies files. Build it with everything it starts:

```bash
cd ~/unitree_go2
source ~/unitree_ros2/setup.sh
vcs import src < go2.repos
colcon build --packages-up-to go2_mapping_bringup
```

`vcs import` fetches Point-LIO, pinned to one commit in `go2.repos`. There are no tests. A launch
file has no logic worth a unit test, and the full run is tried by `map.sh` on a bag.

## Known gaps

- **No test for the launch files.** A typo in a node name or a topic is found only by running the
  chain. The last full run was on the laptop with a bag (`2026-10-19_line_01`), not on the robot.
- **`map_builder_host` must match by hand.** Nothing checks that the two machines have the same value.
  `map_builder` stops a second copy of itself, but a wrong pair that starts none is not noticed.
- **The robot box is still CMU's.** See [go2_preprocessor](./go2_preprocessor.qd).
- **The `RMW_IMPLEMENTATION` default hides a mismatch.** A shell with `rmw_fastrtps_cpp` already set is
  kept, and the two machines then do not see each other, with no error.
- **`laptop.launch.py` starts nothing with `robot`.** It is there for the viewer bridge, which is not
  written yet.
